    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">DiscourseUnit: question.body</rdfs:label>
    <etd:datasetId rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">ai_ethics_datasets</etd:datasetId>
    <etd:runId rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">run-b0eafdda0235eb48</etd:runId>
    <etd:extractedFrom rdf:resource="https://alfie-project.eu/etd-hub#discourse_question.body%7C20"/>
    <etd:contextText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">AI could be dangerous because:&#13;
1) AI systems can have real-world impacts (e.g., biased hiring, facial recognition misuse).&#13;
2) Flawed audits may overlook systemic issues like discrimination, data leakage, or poor model explainability.&#13;
&#13;
What I am asking to explore is:&#13;
A) How to build robust auditing processes (technical and ethical).&#13;
B) Who should conduct audits (internal teams? external bodies? community orgs?).&#13;
C) How to institutionalize ethical review so it’s part of the product lifecycle, not an afterthought.&#13;
D) How to ensure accountability when audit findings are ignored or covered up</etd:contextText>
